Description: CVE-2026-109618 - Avoid state corruption via bailout
 Out-of-memory conditions raised while parsing multipart uploads and running
 the session upload-progress hook (before RINIT) end in zend_bailout(), which
 longjmps back to php_request_startup() and skips cleanup. Persistent globals
 (serializer/unserializer locks, url_scanner state, session globals, http
 globals, user error/exception handlers, objects store, EG(exception),
 EG(in_autoload)) were left pointing at freed request memory, which can be
 turned into memory corruption and code execution.
 .
 This is a PHP 7.4 backport of the relevant parts of upstream php-src commit
 01a40e89 (GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w): stale startup globals are reset on request
 shutdown and early during request startup, the session upload-progress
 handler no longer leaves a half-initialised session behind, and session data
 is (un)serialized in "incomplete classes only" mode before RINIT so that no
 user code (autoload, __wakeup, __unserialize, unserialize_callback_func) is
 executed in that phase. The delayed-OOM allocator framework of the upstream
 change is not backported.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/php/php-src/commit/01a40e89fbfc499936e3b05888d8f00bfde886df (backported to 7.4.3)
Author: Ubuntu Security Backport <security@ubuntu.com>
Last-Update: 2026-10-25
